<h2>How we got here - and what's next</h2>
<p class="updated">Last updated September 9, 2026</p>
<ul class="tl">
  <li><div class="date">October 2023</div>DSNY issues the RFP for an official city bin: rat-resistant, mechanized-truck compatible, and about $50 for the most common size.</li>
  <li><div class="date">July 8, 2024</div>The official NYC Bin is unveiled. Manufacturer Otto Environmental Systems wins the concession agreement, keeping prices well below comparable retail bins.</li>
  <li><div class="date">November 12, 2024</div><strong>Containerization rule takes effect:</strong> buildings with 1-9 residential units (plus city agencies, nonprofits, houses of worship, and professional offices in residential buildings) must set out trash in rigid bins of 55 gallons or less with tight-fitting lids. Black bags on the curb are no longer allowed for these buildings.</li>
  <li><div class="date">January 3, 2025</div><strong>Fines begin</strong> for trash set out without a lidded bin: $50 first offense, $100 second, $200 third and beyond. Any secure-lidded bin still qualifies at this stage.</li>
  <li><div class="date">June 1, 2025</div>The Manhattan Community District 9 pilot launches: stationary on-street &quot;Empire Bins&quot; for buildings with 31+ units (optional for 10-30 units) in West Harlem - the model for the coming citywide rollout to large buildings.</li>
  <li><div class="date">Fall 2025</div>NYC Bins go on direct sale at bins.nyc. Supply problems follow: the manufacturer, Otto, is placed in involuntary bankruptcy in May 2026, and bins become hard to find for a stretch.</li>
  <li><div class="date">June 1, 2026</div><strong>Official NYC Bins become mandatory for trash</strong> at all 1-9 unit properties. A summer-long warning period replaces fines while supply recovers.</li>
  <li><div class="date">September 7, 2026</div>Warning period ends (Labor Day). The STAR homeowner bin reimbursement window also closes this week.</li>
  <li><div class="date">September 8, 2026</div><strong>Full enforcement begins.</strong> DSNY starts issuing $50 summonses for trash set out in anything but an official NYC Bin.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What's coming for larger buildings</h2>
<p>Buildings with <strong>10-30 units</strong> will choose between official NYC Bins and stationary on-street Empire Bins. Buildings with <strong>31+ units</strong> will be required to use Empire Bins - assigned to the building, opened by key card, and emptied by DSNY's new automated side-loading trucks. The citywide containerization program for these buildings is being phased in, with full implementation targeted by <strong>June 1, 2032</strong>.</p>
<p class="src">Sources: <a href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/dsny/collection/containerization/residential-containerization.page">DSNY Residential Containerization</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/dsny/news/26-035/starting-sept-8-residential-properties-1-9-units-must-use-official-nyc-bins-face-50">DSNY Press Release #26-035</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.nyc.gov/assets/dsny/downloads/about/adopted-rules/dsny-final-rule-use-certain-receptacles-entities-dsny-collection-service-090424.pdf">Adopted rule (PDF)</a></p>
